{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "bandup",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-03-25",
  // nodejs_compat is required by the Next.js runtime on Workers.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "limits": {
    // Paid Workers allow this explicit 30-second ceiling. It replaces the
    // Free plan's 10 ms ceiling that terminated ordinary Next.js renders.
    "cpu_ms": 30000
  },
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "BANDUP_DB",
      "database_name": "bandup-data-production",
      "database_id": "50c3ec03-7ee6-446d-b628-ae04c880cdc0",
      "migrations_dir": "cloudflare/migrations"
    }
  ],
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "BANDUP_FILES",
      "bucket_name": "bandup-files-production"
    }
  ],
  "ai": {
    "binding": "AI"
  },
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "AUDIO_GENERATION_RATE_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "99041001",
      "simple": { "limit": 2, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "MODEL_FETCH_RATE_LIMITER",
      "namespace_id": "99041002",
      "simple": { "limit": 12, "period": 60 }
    }
  ],

  /*
    Do not delete the Worker's variables on the way past.

    Wrangler's default is to treat this file as the only source of truth for
    environment configuration — "like a terraform file", as its own schema puts
    it — so every deploy first deletes every plain-text variable on the Worker
    and then sets whatever `vars` says here. Historically there was no `vars`
    block, so the second half set nothing and the first half deleted
    everything. The tiny block below now contains only an explicit production
    safety kill-switch; dashboard variables remain authoritative for the rest.

    That is not a theoretical footgun. It has now taken production down twice:
    once eating ADMIN_USERNAME, and once eating ADMIN_USERNAME again together
    with ACCOUNTS_ENABLED and all six Stripe price ids — which locked the owner
    out of their own admin account and closed subscriptions on a live site,
    silently, on a deploy that reported success. Encrypted Secret values were
    untouched both times, which is what made it look random.

    DEPLOY.md used to answer this by telling whoever set a variable to mark it
    Secret even when it held nothing secret. That worked and was the wrong fix:
    it asked a person to remember a rule at the exact moment the dashboard is
    offering them the sensible-looking default, forever, and the cost of one
    lapse is a broken production site with no error anywhere.

    So the trap goes instead. With this set, the dashboard is authoritative for
    variables and a deploy leaves them alone. Nothing about which values are
    secret changes — a key still belongs in a Secret, because Secrets are
    encrypted at rest and hidden from the dashboard afterwards. What changes is
    that getting that wrong is no longer a way to delete your configuration.

    tests/deploy-config.test.mjs fails if this is removed.
  */
  "keep_vars": true,
  "vars": {
    "ORGANIZATION_UI_PREVIEW": "0",
    // Supabase Auth and application-data reads remain authoritative while
    // every committed learner/profile/progress/billing write is mirrored to
    // D1/R2. Cut reads over only after the migration verifier proves parity.
    "CLOUDFLARE_DATA_MODE": "dual",
    "ORGANIZATION_DATA_MODE": "cloudflare"
  }
}
